From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jakob Linke <jgruber@chromium.org>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 13:31:24 +0200
Subject: [regexp] Don't share named-capture groups objects via the results
 cache

SearchRegExpMultiple caches global-replace results for large
internalized subjects. Both the copy stored into the cache and the copy
handed back on a hit are shallow, so the per-match argument arrays - and
the named-capture groups objects they hold - are shared by identity
across every call that touches the entry. A replace callback receives
that object and can retain and mutate it, and later calls then observe
the mutation instead of the fresh object RegExpBuiltinExec is specified
to produce.

Copy the argument arrays and their groups objects on both sides of the
cache. Copying on entry keeps the cached arrays unreachable from any
callback, which is what lets the cache-hit path clone the groups objects
rather than rebuild them property by property; rebuilding them measured
~2x slower than cloning on a named-capture replace microbenchmark.
Patterns without named captures are unaffected, and their cached entries
hold only immutable values.

Fixed: 514165662
Change-Id: Ifa72a2b1cd64af517464566f500fc73f61820b10
Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/v8/v8/+/8194264
Reviewed-by: Patrick Thier <pthier@chromium.org>
Commit-Queue: Jakob Linke <jgruber@chromium.org>
